-- title:
--   Existence and uniqueness of primitives along a path
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra structure, and assume there is an $x \in F$ transcendental over $\mathbb{C}$ with $F$ finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $\mathbb{C}(x)$ generated by $x$. Assume `IsCurveOver ℂ F`: every nonzero $f \in F$ has a divisor of degree $0$ whose value at each place $v$ is $v.\mathrm{ord}(f) = -\log$ of the adic valuation of $f$, every place has residue field finite over $\mathbb{C}$, and $\Omega[F\!\restriction\!\mathbb{C}]$ is free of rank $1$ over $F$. The space `Place ℂ F` of places (proper valuation subrings of $F$ containing $\mathbb{C}$ that are principal ideal rings) is assumed to carry a Hausdorff topology and the structure of an analytic manifold charted by $\mathbb{C}$, and the hypothesis `hF` requires that for each nonzero $f$ and each place $v$ the function $z \mapsto \mathrm{evalAt}(\varphi_v^{-1}(z), f)$, where $\varphi_v$ is the extended chart at $v$ and $\mathrm{evalAt}$ is residue-field evaluation (set to $0$ off the valuation subring), is meromorphic at $\varphi_v(v)$ with meromorphic order there equal to $v.\mathrm{ord}(f)$. Let $\eta \in \Omega[F\!\restriction\!\mathbb{C}]$, let $P, Q$ be places and $\gamma$ a path from $P$ to $Q$, and suppose $0 \le \mathrm{ord}_{\gamma(t)}(c_{\gamma(t)}(\eta))$ for every $t$ in the unit interval, where $c_v(\eta)$ is the coefficient of $\eta$ with respect to $v.\mathrm{dCoord}$. The conclusion is twofold: there exists $g : [0,1] \to \mathbb{C}$ which is a primitive of $\eta$ along $\gamma$, i.e. for each $t_0$ there is $\Phi : \mathbb{C} \to \mathbb{C}$ having derivative $\mathrm{chartRead}_{\gamma(t_0)}(c_{\gamma(t_0)}(\eta))(z) \cdot \mathrm{chartRead}_{\gamma(t_0)}(\mathrm{dCoordFn})'(z)$ at all $z$ near $\varphi_{\gamma(t_0)}(\gamma(t_0))$ and with $g(t) = \Phi(\varphi_{\gamma(t_0)}(\gamma(t)))$ for all $t$ near $t_0$; and any two primitives $g, g'$ of $\eta$ along $\gamma$ satisfy $g(t) - g'(t) = g(0) - g'(0)$ for all $t$.
--
--   This is the analytic continuation of local primitives along a compact path, in the form needed for differentials regular only along the path rather than everywhere, so that differentials with poles off the path may be integrated; together with the uniqueness clause it makes the path integral $\int_\gamma \eta = g(1) - g(0)$ well defined. It is used in the treatment of periods and residues on the curve, in particular in the cell-dissection computations of path integrals as sums of periods and residues and in the reciprocity statements for integrals along loops.
